import Base from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<Base title="The Build — AP CS Principles (Unity)" active="projects" root="" docsHome="docs/index.html" css="style.css"
  description="Everything you build this year: the starter-project RPG, the boss fight, three game jams, and the AP Create performance task.">
<h1>The Build</h1>

<p>
    All year you build real, shippable software. First semester is one long
    project: your fork of <strong>cs-curriculum-main</strong> grows from a
    walking hero into a full game — a 2D RPG that becomes a 2D platformer
    when you enter a cave — ending with a boss fight that is entirely yours.
    Second semester you fork the <strong>Card-Game-Template</strong> for the
    card jam, and everything converges on the
    <a href="exam/create-performance-task.html">AP Create performance task</a>.
    Push to GitHub every single class.
</p>

<h2>Phase 1 — The Overworld (Sep-Nov)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>PlayerController.cs (v1)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 1.3-1.6</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        The script that runs the year: serialized speed fields, Input.GetAxis
        for direction, direction × speed × Time.deltaTime, your first movement
        method, and the animation wiring (IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack). In 2.2
        you discover that method walks straight through walls, and choose a
        physics-respecting replacement you can defend.
        <a href="lessons/1.4.html">Build it →</a>
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Colliders, stats &amp; the pickup family</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 2.1-2.4</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        The unit where the physics engine switches on: nothing in the project had
        a collider or a rigidbody before it. Coins tick up on trigger pickups,
        spikes knock health down on collision, potions heal with a max-health cap
        (your first <code>if</code>), and death throws you back. Coins and health
        live on <code>PlayerController</code> for now — Debug.Log is your HUD until Unit 5.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>TurretController.cs + Projectile.cs</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 3.1-3.4</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Your first real adversary: a detection radius built from
        Vector3.Distance, rate-limited shooting with a cooldown timer, and
        projectiles that Instantiate, fly, and Destroy. Difficulty lives in
        serialized fields you tune in playtests.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>EnemyController.cs (patrol, chase, attack, return)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 4.1-4.5</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        The full state machine: a patrol you design yourself, an aggro radius
        that flips it to chase, a close-range attack on a damage cooldown, and
        the fall-back to patrol when you escape. Three distances, three
        behaviors — compound conditionals you can draw as a diagram, then
        upgrade to named states.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>AxeItem, the door, and the cave</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 5.1-5.3</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Item pickups set inventory bools and swap your whole animation set; the
        lever's UnityEvents open a door somebody else already wired; and then
        SceneManager.LoadScene exposes a real problem — every stat you own resets
        on scene load. Unit 5 ends with the year's biggest refactor: your stats
        move out of <code>PlayerController</code> into a persistent
        <code>StatManager</code>, and the HUD moves into the scene the game
        actually begins in.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 2 — The Cave, Boss &amp; 3-Button Jam (Nov-Jan)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>PlayerController.cs + Platform.cs + StatManager.cs — jump, lifts, ladders &amp; double jump</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 6.1-6.5</span><span>Type: Starter Project</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>AddForceY()</code> impulses with tuned <code>gravityScale</code>,
        <code>CompareTag("Ground")</code> collision counting (<code>jumpsRemaining</code>),
        <code>Platform.cs</code> waypoint lifts (<code>MoveTowards()</code> + <code>SetParent()</code>),
        Unit 7 ladder triggers that toggle <code>isPlatformer</code>, and moving
        <code>jumpsRemaining</code> onto <code>StatManager</code> for double jumps.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Your cave level (tilemaps)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 7.1-7.7</span><span>Type: Level Design</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        A multi-area cave built with the Cave tile palette, moving platforms, and ladders:
        collision tags, sorting layers, flow, difficulty ramp, and boss arena prep.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>BossController — YOUR independent boss fight</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 8.1-8.6 (Dec 1 - Dec 17)</span><span>Type: Your design</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Two weeks between Thanksgiving and Winter Break building a boss on your own by
        reusing game parts you already built (<code>EnemyController</code>,
        <code>TurretController</code>, projectiles, <code>Platform</code>,
        <code>Spawner</code>, <code>StatManager</code>): brainstorm ideas, prototype fast,
        self-playtest, have friends playtest cold, and revise before break.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 1: The 3-Button Game</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Jan 4 - Jan 21 (Lessons 10.1a-10.1f)</span><span>Type: Game Jam + Sem 1 Showcase</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Returning from Winter Break, January closes Semester 1 with a three-week game jam
        under one strict rule: <strong>at most three buttons of input</strong>. Expands the
        full brainstorm → greybox prototype → self-playtest → friend playtest → revise loop
        and ends with the Jan 21 Semester 1 Final Showcase.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 3 — Data &amp; Systems (Jan-Feb)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Playtest analysis (data/playtests.csv)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Lessons 9.4-9.6</span><span>Type: Data Analysis</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Your own playtest data as a dataset: what happened in your cave,
        filtered, summarized, and argued — plus the bias and ethics roundtable
        that the AP exam loves to ask about.
        <a href="data/playtests.csv">Download the dataset →</a>
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 4 — Spring Jam Season (Feb-Apr)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Spring Micro-Jam Sprint</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Feb 26 - Mar 15</span><span>Type: Game Jam</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        A two-week open micro-jam bridging Unit 9 into Card Game Jam: apply your
        brainstorm → prototype → playtest → revise loop to a fast student-chosen constraint.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 2: The Card Game (your fork of Card-Game-Template)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Mar 17 - Apr 9</span><span>Type: Game Jam + CSP Core</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Lists, arrays, and ScriptableObjects: Card_data assets define your
        cards, GameManager holds decks and hands as Lists, and you fill in
        Shuffle(), Deal(), and AI_Turn(). A deck of cards is the best list
        on the AP exam — you will have built one.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Phase 5 — The Create PT (Apr)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Your Create PT program + video + PPR</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Apr 13 - May 3</span><span>Type: Section II — 30% of AP score</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        An individual program with a list, a parameterized procedure, and an
        algorithm with sequencing, selection, and iteration — built in 9
        in-class hours, captured in a video, and summarized in your
        Personalized Project Reference. Submitted to the AP Digital Portfolio
        by <strong>April 30, 11:59 PM ET</strong>.
        <a href="exam/create-performance-task.html">The full requirements →</a>
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Season 2 — After the exam (May-Jun)</h2>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 3: The Grid/Symmetry Game</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>May 17 - May 27</span><span>Type: Game Jam</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Post-exam: build a game around a grid (match-3, 2048, minesweeper,
        tactics) or around symmetry (mirrored levels, twin-stick echoes).
        You know more than you think — prove it.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="project-file">
    <h3>Jam 4: Placeholder (topic TBA)</h3>
    <div class="file-meta">
        <span>Jun 1 - Jun 3</span><span>Type: Flex jam</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        A fourth jam runs here if the year allows — topic picked by the
        class. If snow days or slow jams eat the calendar, these days
        absorb the shift. The year ends with a retro: your September commit
        vs. your June build, side by side.
    </p>
</div>
</Base>
